[49.149586, "o", "Equal contribution 1 Duke University, Durham, USA. Correspondence to: Yueqian Lin <yl768@duke.edu>. This work\r\nICML Workshop on Machine Learning for Audio, Vancouver,\r\nCanada. Copyright 2025 by the author(s).\r\n(converted to speech) and CN-College-Listen (from\r\n62.86% (CN-College-Listen*).\r\nTable 2: Performance comparison on SPIRAL, DREAMTTS (DTTS), and CN-College-Listen (CCL) benchmark\r\nJ. Peng, Y. Wang, Y. Xi, X. Li, and K. Yu, “A survey on speech large language models,” arXiv preprint\r\narXiv:2410.18908, 2024.\r\nY. Chu et al., “Qwen2-audio technical report,” arXiv\r\npreprint arXiv:2407.10759, 2024.\r\nW. Held et al., “Distilling an end-to-end voice assistant without instruction training data,” arXiv preprint\r\narXiv:2410.02678, 2024.\r\nB. Wang et al., “Audiobench: A universal benchmark for audio large language models,” arXiv preprint\r\narXiv:2406.16020, 2024.\r\ncomputational complexity of self-attention,” in Proceedings\r\nof The 34th International Conference on Algorithmic Learning Theory, S. Agrawal and F. Orabona, Eds., ser. PMLR,\r\nY. Shang, M. Cai, B. Xu, Y. J. Lee, and Y. Yan, “Llavaprumerge: Adaptive token reduction for efficient large multimodal models,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2403.15388, 2024.\r\naudio language model,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2405.08295,\r\nser. Proceedings of Machine Learning Research, vol. 162,\r\nmodel insights,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2402.16363, 2024.\r\n"]
